akes  were much more likely to overeat ice  cream suggesting that a pre load of bad  food triggered them to forego their diet  and eat as much as possible knowing that  restriction would soon be following the  non dieters easily regulated they're  eating and stopped when they were  satisfied on the other hand the dieters  were unable to regulate their intake  which is a process called counter  regulation the researchers determined  that encouraging the dieters to overeat  cause them to forego their restrictive  inhibitions and as a result overeat this  overeating effect by dieters is known as  the what the hell effect essentially the  dieters believe that since they've  broken the rules of their diet they  might as well just go and overdo and eat  all the things in another study  researchers found that overeating was a  combination of restraint and impulsivity  if a person restricted food and it  exacerbated their drive for the food I a  ka increased impulsiveness then they  were more likely to overeat it a good  way to demonstrate this is a seesaw  example on the one end of the seesaw we  have deprivation and restriction and on  the other end of the seesaw  we have guilt when we're on a diet we  restrict and restrict which causes one  end of the seesaw to go up higher and  higher on the other hand we feel less  guilty because we're not eating any of  the bad foods so as deprivation goes up  guilt goes down but if you've ever been  on a seesaw you know that it reaches a  limit deprivation can only go so high  before it comes crashing back down once  it does we see guilt go flying up in  other words you feel like it because you  caved this is why I  hardly ever sustainable it creates this  up-and-down effect that causes chaos in  our mind and body however let's say you  balance out your deprivation and  restriction you don't restrict anything  but you're in tune with your body's true  needs this also balances out your  feelings of guilt because you no longer  are feeling bad about over indulging and  you're not flying high on the fact that  you're eating perfectly as a result  you're in balance so how do you make  peace with food now I know you're  thinking okay Abbi that's great and all  but how do I even start  the answer is to give yourself 100%  unconditional permission to eat no  restrictions no rules just pure  permission I also want a flag that well  this sounds super scary and also like  how the hell can this be healthy I'm  going to just eat chips and ice cream  all day long and a dietitian is saying  that is cool know that this is not all  intuitive eating is to believe that is  completely misconstruing the concept and  the research that supports them  intuitive eating has ten important  principles that build on one another and  we need to reject the diet mentality and  make peace with food so you can rekindle  your trust with your food and body  before we can implement gentle nutrition  guidelines this is really important and  we will get there but there are a few  important steps that we need to get  through first until you 100% believe  that you're allowed any food you will  always feel a sense of deprivation even  if there isn't physical deprivation  happening so what does this look like  well first of all we need to toss out  the idea that there are morally good  foods and bad foods I will not deny that  foods may not be nutritionally equal I'm  a dietician I mean I know way too much  about the fiber and fat content of foods  but if we make them morally equal we're  less likely to obsess over them and we  can better stay in control and second we  need to get rid of any strict conditions  surrounding food so for example thinking  oh I'll eat this today but I'll have to  work out another hour tomorrow as soon  as you start allowing yourself food  freedom you take away the power food has  on you and you  really decrease the chances of  overeating so are you going to overeat  at first yeah I mean probably as you are 