uced a memory for this  food is created and activated to a high  level called the a1 state however the  men  eventually starts to decay over time and  enters an 8 - state if a food is  introduced shortly after when it's in  the a 2 state the stimuli for this food  won't be as strong  however if enough time has gone by that  the person has limited memory of the  food then they go right back to the  excited state of this food and therefore  have a higher drive to eat it what I'm  saying is by allowing yourself regular  access to all the foods they kind of  lose their novelty and you don't feel  such a drive to overeat them also keep  in mind that we constantly fall victim  to self-fulfilling prophecies simply  believing that you're going to fail  almost always leads to our perception of  us failing so believing that being  around chocolate will cause you to  overindulge may cause you to overindulge  you need to develop trust within  yourself that you can do this you are  capable of listening to your body this  intuitive eating journey is all about  rebuilding your relationship with both  food and yourself now one question I  know I'm gonna get is what if you only  want junk food now first of all part of  this exercise or step in the intuitive  eating protocol is to try to not think  of it as junk food the goal in the stage  of intuitive eating training is to treat  all food exactly the same so well I know  that it's in our culture's vocabulary  the best thing you can do is ditch that  kind of talk when we get to gentle  nutrition and honoring our health we  will do a deeper dive into which foods  better support our physical and mental  well-being second when you're given free  choice you may feel like you have an  all-access ticket to eat unhealthy food  and you do now you may enjoy some extra  fun foods for the first little bit but  over time you're likely gonna start to  crave variety and what legitimately  feels good to your body we will be  chatting more about this in an upcoming  episode so definitely stay tuned for  that  now Abby giving up structure is way too  overwhelming for me can we tackle this  in baby steps yes I know that this is  super scary for people so sometimes I do  recommend some baby steps to help  neutralize these trigger foods as we  sometimes call them breaking it down  into some baby bites  this feels a lot more manageable than  the lofty idea of just throwing caution  to the wind on everything so choose one  food to start with and let's attack this  with some structure in my book the  mindful go cookbook I disclose that my  trigger food used to be sugary cereal if  it was in the house I would eat it  mindlessly all day long so as a result I  just wouldn't buy it out of fear that I  would lose control so my intuitive  eating journey with it started with me  buying some cereal then I told myself I  could have cereal every single day if I  wanted and I did I did want cereal the  first day I found I had my breakfast  with cereal and I still wanted to kind  of snack on it later on in the day just  like out of the bag but soon enough I  actually didn't have that desire to  snack on it and I wasn't even that  excited in the morning anymore to have  my sugary cereal breakfast it very  quickly just became another food one I  enjoyed but not one that I was like  obsessed over and I started to implement  some gentle nutrition I noticed that I  was hungry soon after having my cereal  so I started to mix it with some brand  cereal or some Greek yogurt to really up  the satiety factor and then honestly the  next time we just ran out of sugary  cereal in the house I kind of just  forgot to buy it again it lost its power  it really was no longer so enticing once  you've had success neutralizing your big  trigger food you'll maybe have a little  bit more new pham confidence that this  can be applied to other foods that you  have either consciously or unconsciously  restricted or you're viewing through a  moral lens for this week's homework I  want you to start reintroducing foods  that you've previously restricted here's  how number one write down a list that  are appealing to you number two check  off any foods that you actually allow  yourself to eat regularly and circle any  foods that you've restricted in the past  or present number three  plan to allow yourself to eat one of  those forbidden foods as much as you  want either at home or in the store or  in a restaurant wherever you want number  four  while you eat the food check in with  yourself think is this as good as I  remember if it is then of course  continue to allow yourself to have it  now on if it's not which honestly is  common then it could be that your  history of deprivation has caused you to  put it on a pedestal in the first place  this is how I feel personally about  Halloween candy I used to crave it all  the time when I was restricting sugar  but it literally does not taste good to  me anymore and I can easily have it in  the house and ignore it you can start  with just one food if you're more  comfortable before tackling another one  on your list or you can go all in if  that feels easier to you everyone's  going to be different I want this to be  your journey and to take it at the pace  that you're comfortable with and if it  gets too scary then you just need to  take a step back that is okay too you  are not failing even proving to yourself  that you no longer are obsessed with  cheese or cupcakes or cookies is usually  really good data to prove that other  foods also don't deserve so much power  either now before I finish I just want  to highlight something really really  important a lot of criticism around  intuitive eating is that it promotes  overeating and overindulgence of food  the idea of intuitive eating is to make  peace with food and to welcome any kind  of food into your diet it's not about  overdoing it and eating whatever you  want whenever you want and however much  you want but here's the difference it's  actually about listening to your body's  needs you have permission to eat  whatever you want but doing so without  listening to how your body feels will  not be a satisfying experience sometimes  some emotional eating is normal and the  key here is to not punish yourself to  fall back into that binge restrict  repeat cycle but if you're eating beyond  your needs all the time or you're  relying too heavily on food as an  emotional coping mechanism  that's not supporting your health either  I mean it's not a fun experience when  you're sick to your stomach and  ultimately food does not solve all of  our problems this is a situation where I  would suggest speaking to a therapist  even if you're not physically  restricting food and just seemingly  overeating there may be some  psychological restriction going on and  trying to force your relationship with  food into a neutral zone without  addressing some of the  we'll likely keep you trapped in the  diet cycle  remember that intuitive eating takes  time to learn and it's really about  respecting your body's natural cues so  continue to work on these steps  throughout the intuitive eating journey  and stay tuned for our next video on  principle number four challenge the food  police so guys as always I hope you  enjoyed this video if you did be sure to  give it the thumbs up leave me a comment  below if you have any questions about  intuitive eating or any other kind of  topics that you want me to cover in an  upcoming video subscribe to the channel  and I will see you next time on Abby's  kitchen bye  you
 

